<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-injections-surjections" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>One-to-One and Onto Transformations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Two questions can be asked of any transformation: does it ever send two different
      inputs to the same output, and does it reach every possible output? For matrix
      transformations both questions reduce to facts about the matrix that we already know
      how to check.
    </p>
    <p>
      Several figures in this section are interactive. Click and drag the head of the
      input vector <m>\vec{x}</m> or the output vector <m>\vec{b}</m> to explore the
      transformation; drag inside a three-dimensional pane to rotate it. The
      <m>\vec{x}</m> handle can also be moved with the keyboard (press
      <c>Tab</c> to reach it, then the arrow keys), and the
      <q>animate</q> button plays a short demonstration. The deep wine objects always
      show the solution set of <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>, and the olive green objects show
      the range of <m>T</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-injections">
    <title>One-to-One Transformations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-injective">
      <title>Injective (One-to-One) Linear Transformation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^n \to \R^m</m> be a linear transformation, and let
          <m>\vec{x}_1</m> and <m>\vec{x}_2</m> be in <m>\R^n</m>. We say that <m>T</m>
          is an <term>injection</term> or is <term>one-to-one</term> if
          <m>\vec{x}_1 \neq \vec{x}_2</m> implies
          <m>T(\vec{x}_1) \neq T(\vec{x}_2)</m>. Equivalently, if
          <m>T(\vec{x}_1) = T(\vec{x}_2)</m>, then <m>\vec{x}_1 = \vec{x}_2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <figure xml:id="fig-injection-schematic">
      <caption>
        Which transformation is an injection? On the left, two different inputs share an
        output, so the transformation is not one-to-one. On the right, distinct inputs
        have distinct outputs.
      </caption>
      <image width="82%">
        <prefigure label="pf-injection-schematic" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(520,220)" margins="8">
            <coordinates bbox="(0,0,10.4,4.4)">
              <group at="schematic">
                <group at="left-pair">
                  <ellipse at="left-domain" center="(1.3,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                  <ellipse at="left-codomain" center="(4.2,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                  <group at="left-inputs">
                    <point p="(1.3,3.1)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(1.3,2.2)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(1.3,1.3)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                  </group>
                  <group at="left-outputs">
                    <point p="(4.2,3.0)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(4.2,1.2)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                  </group>
                  <group at="left-arrows">
                    <line endpoints="((1.5,3.1),(4.0,3.0))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
                    <line endpoints="((1.5,2.2),(4.0,1.2))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
                    <line endpoints="((1.5,1.3),(4.0,3.0))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
                  </group>
                  <label at="left-label" p="(2.75,0.15)" alignment="n"><m>\text{not one-to-one}</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="right-pair">
                  <ellipse at="right-domain" center="(6.6,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                  <ellipse at="right-codomain" center="(9.5,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                  <group at="right-inputs">
                    <point p="(6.6,3.0)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(6.6,1.3)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                  </group>
                  <group at="right-outputs">
                    <point p="(9.5,3.1)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(9.5,2.2)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(9.5,1.2)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                  </group>
                  <group at="right-arrows">
                    <line endpoints="((6.8,3.0),(9.3,3.1))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
                    <line endpoints="((6.8,1.3),(9.3,1.2))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
                  </group>
                  <label at="right-label" p="(8.05,0.15)" alignment="n"><m>\text{one-to-one}</m></label>
                </group>
              </group>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="schematic" text="Two arrow diagrams, each with a domain oval on its left and a codomain oval on its right; the first transformation is not one-to-one and the second is one-to-one">
                <annotation ref="left-pair" text="First diagram, labelled not one-to-one">
                  <annotation ref="left-domain" text="The domain oval"/>
                  <annotation ref="left-inputs" text="Three inputs in the domain: top, middle and bottom"/>
                  <annotation ref="left-codomain" text="The codomain oval"/>
                  <annotation ref="left-outputs" text="Two outputs in the codomain: upper and lower"/>
                  <annotation ref="left-arrows" text="Three arrows: the top input and the bottom input both go to the upper output, and the middle input goes to the lower output; two different inputs share an output"/>
                  <annotation ref="left-label" text="The label not one-to-one"/>
                </annotation>
                <annotation ref="right-pair" text="Second diagram, labelled one-to-one">
                  <annotation ref="right-domain" text="The domain oval"/>
                  <annotation ref="right-inputs" text="Two inputs in the domain: upper and lower"/>
                  <annotation ref="right-codomain" text="The codomain oval"/>
                  <annotation ref="right-outputs" text="Three outputs in the codomain: top, middle and bottom"/>
                  <annotation ref="right-arrows" text="Two arrows: the upper input goes to the top output and the lower input goes to the bottom output; distinct inputs have distinct outputs, and the middle output is not used"/>
                  <annotation ref="right-label" text="The label one-to-one"/>
                </annotation>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-injective-iff-trivial">
      <title>One-to-One and the Trivial Solution</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m \times n</m> matrix and let <m>\vec{x}</m> be a vector
          of length <m>n</m>. Then the transformation induced by <m>A</m>, <m>T_A</m>, is
          one-to-one (1) if and only if <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> implies
          <m>\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> (2).
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          The statement has two directions, and each needs its own argument.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>First direction.</term>
          <em>Assume</em> that <m>T_A</m> is one-to-one. <em>We show</em> that
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> implies <m>\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let <m>\vec{u}</m> be an element of <m>\R^n</m> such that
          <m>A\vec{u} = \vec{0}</m>, that is, <m>T_A(\vec{u}) = \vec{0}</m>. Since
          <m>T_A</m> is a linear transformation, we also have
          <m>T_A(\vec{0}) = \vec{0}</m>
          (<xref ref="thm-linear-transformation-properties" text="type-global" />). So
          <m>T_A(\vec{u}) = T_A(\vec{0})</m>, and because <m>T_A</m> is one-to-one,
          <m>\vec{u} = \vec{0}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Second direction.</term>
          <em>Assume</em> that <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> implies
          <m>\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>. <em>We show</em> that <m>T_A</m> is one-to-one,
          that is, that <m>T_A(\vec{u}) = T_A(\vec{v})</m> implies
          <m>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Let <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> be elements of <m>\R^n</m> such that
          <m>T_A(\vec{u}) = T_A(\vec{v})</m>, that is,
          <m>A\vec{u} = A\vec{v}</m>. Then <m>A(\vec{u} - \vec{v}) = \vec{0}</m>. By
          the assumption, applied to the vector <m>\vec{u} - \vec{v}</m>, this implies
          <m>\vec{u} - \vec{v} = \vec{0}</m>, or <m>\vec{u} = \vec{v}</m>. Hence
          <m>T_A</m> is one-to-one.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="eg-injective-true-false">
      <title>True or False?</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Let <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix,
          and assume that <m>T</m> is injective. Choose <em>True</em> or <em>False</em>
          for each statement, then select <em>Check Me</em> to see feedback. You can
          change your answer and check again.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task xml:id="tf-injective-every-output">
        <statement correct="no">
          <p>
            For every <m>\vec{b} \in \R^m</m>, there exists one solution for the
            equation <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            False. For example, let
            <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
            <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^3</m>, which is a one-to-one transformation. Now, for
            <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>, the system
            <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> has no solution, since the augmented matrix is
            <md>
              \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 3
              \end{array} \right]
              \xrightarrow{r_3 - r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right],
            </md>
            and therefore the system is inconsistent.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </task>
      <task xml:id="tf-injective-trivial-solution">
        <statement correct="yes">
          <p>
            <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only the trivial solution.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            True. See <xref ref="thm-injective-iff-trivial" text="type-global" />.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </task>
      <task xml:id="tf-injective-independent-columns">
        <statement correct="yes">
          <p>
            The columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            True. Note that
            <md>
              A\vec{x} = \vec{0}
              \iff x_1\vec{A}_1 + x_2\vec{A}_2 + \cdots + x_n\vec{A}_n = \vec{0}.
            </md>
            If <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only the trivial solution, it means that none
            of the columns of <m>A</m> can be written as a linear combination of the rest of
            them, since if for example <m>\vec{A}_1</m> could be written as a linear
            combination
            <md>
              \vec{A}_1 = a_2\vec{A}_2 + a_3\vec{A}_3 + \cdots + a_n\vec{A}_n,
            </md>
            this gives
            <m>\vec{A}_1 - a_2\vec{A}_2 - a_3\vec{A}_3 - \cdots - a_n\vec{A}_n = \vec{0}</m>,
            which contradicts the fact that <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only the trivial
            solution.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </task>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-which-are-injections">
      <title>Which Transformation Is an Injection?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Reflection in the <m>x</m>-axis in <m>\R^2</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Projection into the <m>x</m>-axis in <m>\R^2</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>T \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(A)</term> The corresponding matrix is
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and the system
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only the trivial solution, hence this is an
          injection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(B)</term> Similarly,
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, whereas
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has non-trivial solutions, hence this is NOT an
          injection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(C)</term> Again
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}</m>; however,
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has a free parameter and therefore (C) is not an
          injection. For example,
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
            = T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-injective-geometric">
      <title>A One-to-One Transformation, Seen Geometrically</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^3</m> be the matrix transformation
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Is <m>T</m> one-to-one?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Row reducing <m>A</m> gives
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{r_2 - r_1}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{r_3 - r_2}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so there is a pivot in every column. Hence <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has only
          the trivial solution, and <m>T</m> is one-to-one by
          <xref ref="thm-injective-iff-trivial" text="type-global"/>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-injective-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            Click and drag the head of the input vector <m>\vec{x}</m> on the left: different inputs always
            produce different outputs <m>\vec{b} = T(\vec{x})</m> on the right, so
            <m>T</m> is one-to-one. The deep wine dot on the left is the solution set of
            <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>: it is a single point. The olive green plane on the
            right is the range of <m>T</m>.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-injective-geometric" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,0:1,1:0,1&amp;x=-1,2&amp;show=true" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-injective-geometric-light">
          <caption>
            Explore the same transformation with the keyboard: focus the head of
            <m>\vec{x}</m> and use the arrow keys, or drag either vector.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-injective-geometric-light" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,0:1,1:0,1&amp;x=-1,2&amp;show=true" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-not-injective-geometric">
      <title>A Transformation That Is Not One-to-One, Seen Geometrically</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^2</m> be the matrix transformation
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Is <m>T</m> one-to-one?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The matrix <m>A</m> is already in reduced row echelon form, and the third
          column has no pivot. Solving <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> with free variable
          <m>z</m> gives <m>x = -z</m> and <m>y = -z</m>, so the solution set is
          <md>
            \vec{x} = z \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad z \text{ arbitrary}.
          </md>
          Taking <m>z = 1</m> produces the nontrivial solution
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
            = T \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so two different inputs share the output <m>\vec{0}</m>, and <m>T</m> is not
          one-to-one.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-not-injective-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            The deep wine line on the left is the solution set of <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{0}</m>,
            namely all multiples of <m>(-1,-1,1)</m>. Click and drag the head of <m>\vec{x}</m> along this
            line: the output <m>T(\vec{x})</m> stays at <m>\vec{0}</m>, so <m>T</m>
            sends infinitely many inputs to the same output.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-not-injective-geometric" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,0,1:0,1,1&amp;x=0,0,0&amp;show=true&amp;lock=true" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-wide-never-injective">
      <title>Wide Matrices Are Never One-to-One</title>
      <p>
        If <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix with more columns than rows
        (<m>n \gt m</m>), then <m>A</m> cannot have a pivot in every column: there is
        at most one pivot per row, and there are fewer rows than columns. Hence
        <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has a free variable, and the transformation
        <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is <em>never</em> one-to-one. This is why the
        transformation <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^2</m> in the previous example had no chance:
        a transformation from a bigger space to a smaller space must collapse
        different inputs together.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-surjections">
    <title>Onto Transformations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-surjective">
      <title>Surjection (Onto)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^n \to \R^m</m> be a linear transformation. We say that <m>T</m>
          is a <term>surjection</term> or <term>onto</term> if, for every
          <m>\vec{b} \in \R^m</m>, there exists an <m>\vec{x} \in \R^n</m> such that
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <figure xml:id="fig-surjection-schematic">
      <caption>
        Which transformation is a surjection? On the left every element of the codomain is
        hit; on the right one element is missed, so the transformation is not onto.
      </caption>
      <image width="82%">
        <prefigure label="pf-surjection-schematic" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(520,220)" margins="8">
            <coordinates bbox="(0,0,10.4,4.4)">
              <group at="schematic">
                <group at="left-pair">
                  <ellipse at="left-domain" center="(1.3,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                  <ellipse at="left-codomain" center="(4.2,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                  <group at="left-inputs">
                    <point p="(1.3,3.1)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(1.3,2.2)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(1.3,1.3)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                  </group>
                  <group at="left-outputs">
                    <point p="(4.2,3.0)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(4.2,1.2)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                  </group>
                  <group at="left-arrows">
                    <line endpoints="((1.5,3.1),(4.0,3.0))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
                    <line endpoints="((1.5,2.2),(4.0,1.2))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
                    <line endpoints="((1.5,1.3),(4.0,3.0))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
                  </group>
                  <label at="left-label" p="(2.75,0.15)" alignment="n"><m>\text{onto}</m></label>
                </group>
                <group at="right-pair">
                  <ellipse at="right-domain" center="(6.6,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                  <ellipse at="right-codomain" center="(9.5,2.2)" axes="(1.0,1.9)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
                  <group at="right-inputs">
                    <point p="(6.6,3.0)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(6.6,1.3)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                  </group>
                  <group at="right-outputs">
                    <point p="(9.5,3.1)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(9.5,2.2)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                    <point p="(9.5,1.2)" fill="#4A5D23" size="4"/>
                  </group>
                  <group at="right-arrows">
                    <line endpoints="((6.8,3.0),(9.3,3.1))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
                    <line endpoints="((6.8,1.3),(9.3,1.2))" stroke="#5E011C" thickness="1" arrows="1"/>
                  </group>
                  <label at="right-label" p="(8.05,0.15)" alignment="n"><m>\text{not onto}</m></label>
                </group>
              </group>
            </coordinates>
            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="schematic" text="Two arrow diagrams, each with a domain oval on its left and a codomain oval on its right; the first transformation is onto and the second is not onto">
                <annotation ref="left-pair" text="First diagram, labelled onto">
                  <annotation ref="left-domain" text="The domain oval"/>
                  <annotation ref="left-inputs" text="Three inputs in the domain: top, middle and bottom"/>
                  <annotation ref="left-codomain" text="The codomain oval"/>
                  <annotation ref="left-outputs" text="Two outputs in the codomain: upper and lower"/>
                  <annotation ref="left-arrows" text="Three arrows: the top input and the bottom input go to the upper output, and the middle input goes to the lower output; every element of the codomain is hit"/>
                  <annotation ref="left-label" text="The label onto"/>
                </annotation>
                <annotation ref="right-pair" text="Second diagram, labelled not onto">
                  <annotation ref="right-domain" text="The domain oval"/>
                  <annotation ref="right-inputs" text="Two inputs in the domain: upper and lower"/>
                  <annotation ref="right-codomain" text="The codomain oval"/>
                  <annotation ref="right-outputs" text="Three outputs in the codomain: top, middle and bottom"/>
                  <annotation ref="right-arrows" text="Two arrows: the upper input goes to the top output and the lower input goes to the bottom output; the middle output is missed"/>
                  <annotation ref="right-label" text="The label not onto"/>
                </annotation>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-surjection-simple">
      <title>A Transformation That Is Not Onto</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> be the linear transformation defined by
          <m>T \begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} a + b \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> for all
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \end{bmatrix} \in \R^2</m>. Determine whether <m>T</m>
          is a surjective transformation or not.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Every image has second entry <m>0</m>, so no vector with a nonzero second entry
          is in the range. For instance, <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is
          never an image. Hence <m>T</m> is not a surjection.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-which-are-surjections">
      <title>Which Transformation Is a Surjection?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^2</m> with
                <m>T(\vec{x}) = \begin{bmatrix} x - z \\ y + z \end{bmatrix}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
                <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(I)</term> The matrix is
          <md>
            A = \begin{bmatrix} T(\vec{e}_1) \amp T(\vec{e}_2) \amp T(\vec{e}_3) \end{bmatrix}
              = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          The system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>, or
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          will always have a solution, since
          <m>\left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp b_1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp b_2 \end{array} \right]</m>
          has two leading ones and three variables, one of which is a free variable.
          Hence <m>T</m> is a surjection.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II)</term> The system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>, or
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp b_1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp b_2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp b_3
            \end{array} \right],
          </md>
          has no solution for <m>b_3 \neq 0</m>, which means <m>T</m> is not a surjection.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-surjective-geometric">
      <title>An Onto Transformation, and What One More Measurement Adds</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^2</m> be the matrix transformation of
          <xref ref="eg-not-injective-geometric" text="type-global" />,
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> with
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, so
          that <m>T(x, y, z) = (x + z,\, y + z)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                For an arbitrary output
                <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} u \\ v \end{bmatrix}</m>, describe every
                input <m>\vec{x}</m> with <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>. Is <m>T</m> onto?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                A further instrument records <m>m = px + qy + rz</m>, where <m>p</m>,
                <m>q</m>, <m>r</m> are fixed real numbers. For which <m>p</m>, <m>q</m>,
                <m>r</m> do the three readings <m>u</m>, <m>v</m>, <m>m</m> determine
                the input uniquely?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                In the exceptional case, which values of <m>m</m> are possible, and what
                freedom remains?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Give one informative instrument and one redundant instrument.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a)</term> The augmented matrix of <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>,
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp u \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp v
            \end{array} \right],
          </md>
          is already in reduced row echelon form. There is a pivot in every row, so no
          row of the form
          <m>\left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp d \end{array} \right]</m>
          with <m>d \neq 0</m> can occur: the system is consistent for every <m>u</m> and
          <m>v</m>, and <m>T</m> is onto. The third column has no pivot, so
          <m>z = t</m> is free and
          <md>
            \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} u - t \\ v - t \\ t \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} u \\ v \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
            + t \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad t \in \R.
          </md>
          Every output comes from a whole line of inputs: one particular input plus the
          line of inputs with output <m>\vec{0}</m> found in
          <xref ref="eg-not-injective-geometric" text="type-global" />, exactly as
          <xref ref="thm-particular-plus-homogeneous" text="type-global" /> predicts.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b)</term> The new reading adds a third row:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp u \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp v \\
                p \amp q \amp r \amp m
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{-p\,r_1 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp u \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp v \\
                0 \amp q \amp r - p \amp m - pu
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-q\,r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp u \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp v \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp r - p - q \amp m - pu - qv
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          If <m>r \neq p + q</m>, every column has a pivot and the input is unique:
          <md>
            z = \frac{m - pu - qv}{r - p - q}, \qquad x = u - z, \qquad y = v - z.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c)</term> If <m>r = p + q</m>, the last row is
          <m>\left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp m - pu - qv \end{array} \right]</m>.
          When <m>m \neq pu + qv</m> the system is inconsistent: no input produces those
          three readings. When <m>m = pu + qv</m> the row is zero, and the whole line of
          part (a) remains. So the only possible reading is <m>m = pu + qv</m>, and it
          removes none of the freedom.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(d)</term> Recording <m>z</m> itself, <m>(p, q, r) = (0, 0, 1)</m>, is
          informative, since <m>r \neq p + q</m>: then <m>z = m</m>,
          <m>x = u - m</m>, <m>y = v - m</m>. Recording <m>x + y + 2z</m>,
          <m>(p, q, r) = (1, 1, 2)</m>, is redundant, since <m>r = p + q</m>: this
          reading is always <m>u + v</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The number <m>r - p - q</m> is the reading of the instrument on
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, the direction of the line in
          part (a). An instrument is informative exactly when it does not vanish on that
          direction, that is, when it can tell apart the inputs that <m>T</m> cannot.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-surjective-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            Click and drag the head of the output vector <m>\vec{b}</m> anywhere on the right: the demo
            always finds an input <m>\vec{x}</m> with <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>,
            because every vector in <m>\R^2</m> is an output of <m>T</m>.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-surjective-geometric" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,0,1:0,1,1&amp;x=1,1,.5" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-not-surjective-geometric">
      <title>One-to-One but Not Onto: What the Output Remembers</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^3</m> be the matrix transformation of
          <xref ref="eg-injective-geometric" text="type-global" />,
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> with
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, so
          that <m>T(x, y) = (x,\, x + y,\, y)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Find exactly which vectors
                <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} p \\ q \\ r \end{bmatrix}</m> are outputs of
                <m>T</m>, and recover every input in that case. Is <m>T</m> onto?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Use part (a) to explain again why <m>T</m> is one-to-one.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Suppose one of the three output entries is lost. In each of the three
                cases, decide whether the two remaining entries still determine the
                input, and give formulas for it.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                How can <m>T</m> miss most vectors of <m>\R^3</m> and yet lose no
                information about its input?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a)</term> Row reduce the augmented matrix of
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp p \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp q \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp r
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow{-r_1 + r_2}
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp p \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp q - p \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp r
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_3}
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp p \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp q - p \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp p - q + r
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
          The system is consistent exactly when <m>q = p + r</m>. So the range of
          <m>T</m> is the plane <m>b_2 = b_1 + b_3</m> in <m>\R^3</m>, a two-dimensional
          slice of the three-dimensional codomain. For instance,
          <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> has
          <m>1 \neq 1 + 1</m>, so it is not an output, and <m>T</m> is not onto.
          (Alternatively: <m>A</m> has only two pivots, so it cannot have a pivot in
          every one of its three rows.) When <m>q = p + r</m>, the input is
          <md>
            x = p, \qquad y = q - p = r.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b)</term> Both columns have a pivot, so there is no free variable: every
          output in the range comes from exactly one input. This is the conclusion of
          <xref ref="eg-injective-geometric" text="type-global" />, now with a formula
          for the input.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c)</term> Each case leaves two equations in <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <em>Third entry lost.</em>
                <md>
                  \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp p \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp q \end{array} \right]
                  \xrightarrow{-r_1 + r_2}
                  \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp p \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp q - p \end{array} \right],
                  \qquad (x, y) = (p,\, q - p).
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <em>Second entry lost.</em> The two remaining equations are
                <m>x = p</m> and <m>y = r</m>, so <m>(x, y) = (p,\, r)</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <em>First entry lost.</em>
                <md>
                  \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp 1 \amp q \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp r \end{array} \right]
                  \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_1}
                  \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp q - r \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp r \end{array} \right],
                  \qquad (x, y) = (q - r,\, r).
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
          Each coefficient matrix has two pivots, so the input is determined in every
          case. Any one of the three entries can be lost without losing the input.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(d)</term> The three entries of an output always satisfy
          <m>q = p + r</m>, so they carry only two independent numbers, exactly as many
          as the input has. Being onto is a question about the codomain: here
          <m>\R^3</m> contains vectors off the plane. Being one-to-one is a question
          about the inputs: here no two of them share an output. Neither property
          decides the other.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-not-surjective-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            The range of <m>T</m> is the olive green plane on the right, which is smaller
            than the codomain <m>\R^3</m>. Click and drag the head of <m>\vec{b}</m> off the olive green plane:
            the equation <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> becomes inconsistent, so such a
            <m>\vec{b}</m> is not an output of <m>T</m>.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-not-surjective-geometric" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,0:1,1:0,1&amp;x=1,1" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-tall-never-surjective">
      <title>Tall Matrices Are Never Onto</title>
      <p>
        If <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix with more rows than columns
        (<m>m \gt n</m>), then <m>A</m> cannot have a pivot in every row: there is at
        most one pivot per column, and there are fewer columns than rows. Hence the
        transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is <em>never</em> onto. This is
        the mirror image of <xref ref="rem-wide-never-injective" text="type-global"/>:
        a transformation from a smaller space into a bigger space cannot fill up its
        codomain.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-injective-surjective-together">
    <title>One-to-One and Onto Together</title>

    <p>
      Both properties are read off from the pivots of <m>A</m>:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>T</m> is <em>one-to-one</em> if and only if <m>A</m> has a pivot in
            every <em>column</em>, that is, if and only if the columns of <m>A</m> are
            linearly independent.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>T</m> is <em>onto</em> if and only if <m>A</m> has a pivot in every
            <em>row</em>, that is, if and only if the columns of <m>A</m> span
            <m>\R^m</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      A transformation can have either property without the other, both, or neither.
      For instance, the transformation <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^4</m> of
      <xref ref="eg-attainable-outputs" text="type-global" /> is one-to-one but not
      onto: each vector <m>\vec{b}</m> with <m>b_3 + b_4 = 0</m> is the output of exactly
      one input, and no other vector of <m>\R^4</m> is an output at all.
      The examples below complete the picture.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-neither-injective-nor-surjective">
      <title>A Transformation That Is Neither One-to-One Nor Onto</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^2</m> be the matrix transformation
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 4 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Show that <m>T</m> is neither one-to-one nor onto.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Row reducing,
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 4 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{r_2 - 2r_1}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so <m>A</m> has a single pivot. There is no pivot in the second or third
          column, so <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has two free variables and <m>T</m> is
          not one-to-one: the solution set of <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{0}</m> is the entire
          plane <m>x + 2y - z = 0</m>. There is also no pivot in the second row, so
          <m>T</m> is not onto: the range is
          <m>\operatorname{Span}\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>, a line in
          <m>\R^2</m>, since every column of <m>A</m> is a multiple of
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>. For example,
          <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is not on this line, so
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> has no solution.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-neither-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 4 \amp -2 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            The deep wine plane on the left is the solution set of
            <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> for the current <m>\vec{b}</m>; since it
            contains more than one point, <m>T</m> is not one-to-one. The olive green line
            on the right is the range of <m>T</m>; since it is smaller than
            <m>\R^2</m>, <m>T</m> is not onto. Click and drag the head of <m>\vec{b}</m> off the line to
            make the equation inconsistent.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-neither-geometric" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,2,-1:2,4,-2&amp;x=1,1,1&amp;show=true" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-projection-xy-plane">
      <title>Projection into the <m>xy</m>-Plane Is Neither</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>P : \R^3 \to \R^3</m> be projection into the <m>xy</m>-plane, that is,
          <md>
            P \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Show that <m>P</m> is neither one-to-one nor onto.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First find the matrix. Since <m>P(\vec{e}_1) = \vec{e}_1</m>,
          <m>P(\vec{e}_2) = \vec{e}_2</m> and <m>P(\vec{e}_3) = \vec{0}</m>,
          <md>
            A = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Not one-to-one.</term> The system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> reads
          <m>x = 0</m>, <m>y = 0</m>, and <m>0 = 0</m>, so <m>z</m> is a free variable and
          the solutions are
          <md>
            \vec{x} = t \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \qquad t \in \R,
          </md>
          the whole <m>z</m>-axis. There are nontrivial solutions, so by
          <xref ref="thm-injective-iff-trivial" text="type-global" /> <m>P</m> is not
          one-to-one. Concretely,
          <md>
            P \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = P \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          two different inputs with the same image. This is exactly what projection does:
          it forgets the height <m>z</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Not onto.</term> Every output has third entry <m>0</m>, so the range of
          <m>P</m> is the <m>xy</m>-plane
          <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{e}_1, \vec{e}_2 \}</m>, which is smaller than the
          codomain <m>\R^3</m>. For instance
          <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> has no preimage: the
          third equation of <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> reads <m>0 = 1</m>, so the system is
          inconsistent.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both failures are visible in <m>\det A = 0</m>: by
          <xref ref="thm-invertible-matrix-theorem" text="type-global" />, a square matrix
          that is not invertible is neither one-to-one nor onto, consistent with
          <xref ref="rem-square-injective-iff-surjective" text="type-global" />.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-projection-xy-geometric">
          <caption>
            Projection into the <m>xy</m>-plane, <m>P(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> with
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            The deep wine vertical line on the left is the solution set of
            <m>P(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m>: an entire line of inputs shares one image, so
            <m>P</m> is not one-to-one. The olive green horizontal plane on the right is the
            range of <m>P</m>, which is smaller than <m>\R^3</m>. Click and drag the head
            of <m>\vec{b}</m> off that plane and the equation becomes inconsistent, so
            <m>P</m> is not onto either.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-projection-xy-geometric" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=1,0,0:0,1,0:0,0,0&amp;x=1,1,2&amp;show=true" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-both-injective-and-surjective">
      <title>A Transformation That Is Both One-to-One and Onto</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> be the matrix transformation
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>, where
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Show that <m>T</m> is both one-to-one and onto.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Row reducing,
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_2}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow{r_2 - 2r_1}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
            \xrightarrow[r_1 + r_2]{-r_2}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so <m>A</m> has a pivot in every column (<m>T</m> is one-to-one) and in every
          row (<m>T</m> is onto). In fact, for every <m>\vec{b} \in \R^2</m> the
          equation <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> has <em>exactly one</em> solution,
          <md>
            \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix} \vec{b},
          </md>
          as can be checked by multiplying the two matrices. (We will study such
          <em>invertible</em> matrices in detail later.)
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-both-geometric">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> for
            <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>.
            Click and drag the head of either vector: every output <m>\vec{b}</m> comes from exactly one
            input <m>\vec{x}</m>, so <m>T</m> is one-to-one and onto. The olive green
            region on the right is the range of <m>T</m>, which is all of <m>\R^2</m>.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-both-geometric" iframe="demos-manim/axb.html?mat=2,1:1,1&amp;x=1,1&amp;show=true" width="100%" aspect="5:4"/>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-square-injective-iff-surjective">
      <title>Square Matrices: One-to-One Equals Onto</title>
      <p>
        For an <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, having a pivot in every column is the
        same as having <m>n</m> pivots, which is the same as having a pivot in every
        row. So for a square matrix, the transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is
        one-to-one if and only if it is onto. For non-square matrices the two
        properties are independent <mdash/> except that, by
        <xref ref="rem-wide-never-injective" text="type-global"/> and
        <xref ref="rem-tall-never-surjective" text="type-global"/>, a wide matrix is
        never one-to-one and a tall matrix is never onto.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>
</section>
